course: dictionary
lesson: 10979
title: Warlord: power by force
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A warlord is a military leader. He has his own army and controls an area by force, usually when there is no strong government to stop him. «China was divided among warlords» tells you the country was split into areas, each ruled by a warlord instead of one government.
explain: Look at the word parts: war and lord. A lord is an old word for a powerful ruler, so a warlord is a ruler whose power comes from fighting. «Then China broke apart under warlords» gives the same clue: the country fell into pieces, each under a leader with soldiers and weapons.
explain: Ask yourself who gave this leader power. If the answer is his army, you have found a warlord. Notice the phrases local warlord and rival warlords, and remember that warlord is a noun: one warlord, many warlords.
fact: A warlord is a military leader who controls an area by force, with his own army.
fact: Warlords usually appear when there is no strong government.
fact: «Rival warlords» means warlords who compete against each other for power.
fact: In China after 1916, the country was divided among warlords.
fact: On 4 May 1919, students in Beijing protested, and this started the May Fourth movement.
exercise: «China was divided among warlords.» What are warlords? | Teachers who run schools | Sailors on a ship at sea | *Leaders who control an area by force with their own army | Kings chosen by a vote of the people
exercise: In «rival warlords», what does rival suggest? | *Warlords who compete against each other | Warlords who are best friends | Warlords who are very old and ancient | Warlords who are very quiet and shy
exercise: Ellie says warlords get power by winning elections. Is she right? | Yes, because the people always choose them | *No, a warlord gets power by force, not elections | No, warlords never have an army | Yes, that is how warlords get power
test: What does a warlord use to control an area? | A vote held by the people | Schools and books | Letters sent to a king | *His own army and force
test: The word warlord has war in it. What clue does that give you? | Warlords are children | Warlords are very kind leaders | *Power comes from fighting | A warlord is a type of fruit
exam: Why do warlords appear when there is no strong government? | Because warlords live at sea | Because warlords are elected each year | *Because nobody can easily stop an armed leader who controls the area | Because governments always ask warlords for help with schools
